## So the finalized choices are

| Layer                  | Choice                        |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Control server         | **Hetzner 4c / 8GB**          |
| Containers             | Docker Compose                |
| DB                     | PostgreSQL                    |
| Social/matches/leagues | **Nakama**                    |
| Game execution         | **Pog Runtime**               |
| Game format            | Git + `game.pack.v1`          |
| Character format       | `freak.pack.v1`               |
| Cache/jobs             | Redis                         |
| Assets                 | Cloudflare R2                 |
| Mobile                 | **Expo / React Native**       |
| Browser                | **Expo Web / React**          |
| Realtime media         | **LiveKit**                   |
| 3D/show engine         | **Unreal Engine 5.8**         |
| VR                     | Unreal + OpenXR               |
| Large crowd            | Unreal Mass + LOD             |
| Live GPU               | **Vast 4090-class**           |
| Cheap batch GPU        | Vast / RunPod                 |
| Host intelligence      | Qwen3.5 Omni Realtime         |
| Character TTS          | Qwen3-TTS                     |
| Face                   | Audio2Face 3                  |
| Emotion                | Audio2Emotion                 |
| Music/SFX              | Stable Audio 3 Small          |
| Cinematic direction    | Pog Director → Unreal cameras |
| Massive broadcast      | **Cloudflare Stream WebRTC**  |
| External agents        | HTTP/WebSocket + MCP adapter  |

**I would commit to that architecture.**

The core bet is not really Unreal. It's that **`Freak Pack + Game Pack + Agent Protocol + Event Protocol` are engine-independent**. That lets the lightweight party game exist today, the absurd cinematic show exist on Unreal next, and then VR becomes another client rather than Pog Town v2. Unreal gets chosen because the part it controls—embodied characters, crowds, cameras and virtual production—is exactly the part where its advantages are overwhelming.

